<Page path="start/quickstart">
<section id="quickstart">
    <h2 id="before"><a href="#before">Before you start</a></h2>
  <p>You need a container runtime with <code>docker compose</code>, git, and a <a href="../reference/manifest.html"><code>multiverse.yaml</code></a> (a path or a git URL).</p>
  <h2 id="first"><a href="#first">Your first universe</a></h2>
  <Terminal><pre><span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl start ./multiverse.yaml
<span class="c">› universe 1: a worktree of every repository</span>
<span class="c">› starting universe 1</span>
<span class="k">✓</span> universe 1 is up
  http://api-u1.192-168-1-20.traefik.me</pre></Terminal>
  <p>A repository that is only a compose file needs no <code>multiverse.yaml</code> to try this: <code>multiversectl start acme/api</code> runs it as it is.</p>
  <p>That is the whole of it: <code>start</code> installs the multiverse, makes universe 1 and starts it, and ends on an address to open. The source is anything <code>start</code> takes — <code>owner/repo</code>, a git URL, a path, or a node in your catalog. Run it again and it starts the universe that is already there; with several, <code>-u</code> names one; <code>--open</code> opens the first address in a browser.</p>
  <p>The same thing as the steps it takes once the multiverse is installed, for when you want one of them on its own:</p>
  <Terminal><pre><span class="c"># universe 1: a worktree of every repo</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl universe new 1
<span class="c"># start its containers</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl universe up 1
<span class="c"># list universes and their state</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl universe ls
<span class="c"># print the hostnames universe 1 answers on</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl universe urls 1
<span class="c"># check the host, the multiverse and universe 1</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl universe doctor 1
<span class="c"># stop and delete universe 1 (asks first)</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl universe rm 1</pre></Terminal>
  <h2 id="see"><a href="#see">Seeing what you have</a></h2>
  <p><code>ls</code> is one block per universe: which app, on which branch, at which address.</p>
  <Terminal><pre><span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl universe ls
<span class="k">universe 3</span>  ·  acme-u3  ·  6 running  ·  192-168-1-20.traefik.me
<span class="c">─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</span>
<span class="c">APP    BRANCH  URL</span>
admin  u3      http://adm-u3.192-168-1-20.traefik.me
api    u3      http://api-u3.192-168-1-20.traefik.me
api            http://devapi-u3.192-168-1-20.traefik.me:8001
api            http://mail-u3.192-168-1-20.traefik.me
web    u3      http://app-u3.192-168-1-20.traefik.me
mobile u3      checkout only, no services</pre></Terminal>
  <p class="muted">A host that is also forwarded to a port shows that address beside it. <code>ls -o wide</code> is the rest: the service behind each address, what is up and what is off, what publishes a port, and every worktree, compose project and volume. <code>-o list</code> is one line per universe, for piping, and they combine: <code>-o list,wide</code>.</p>
</section>

<section id="second">
  <h2 id="second"><a href="#second">A second universe</a></h2>
  <p>The point of the tool is the next command, not the first. Create another and the first keeps running:</p>
  <Terminal><pre><span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl universe new fix/login
<span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl universe up fix/login
<span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl universe ls</pre></Terminal>
  <p>Both serve at once on different hostnames, with their own containers, databases and checkouts. Nothing was stopped and no port reassigned — <a href="../concepts/networking.html">Networking</a> explains why.</p>
</section>

<section id="browser">
  <h2 id="browser"><a href="#browser">The same thing in a browser</a></h2>
  <p>Everything above is also on a page — the catalog, the multiverses and every universe, live, with the same operations behind buttons.</p>
  <Terminal><pre><span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl server start --open
<span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl server stop</pre></Terminal>
  <p>It runs in the background behind a token, and the two can be used together. <a href="../tasks/server.html">Explore and run it in a browser</a> has the rest.</p>
</section>
</Page>
